# The accumulator rewrite asked for an integer literal as the work left over
# after the recursive call, so `n + weigh (n - 1)` kept a frame per call and
# died past the interpreter's ten-thousand-frame guard. The operand here is a
# parameter the wrapper has already ascribed `int`, and every recursive call
# hands that position another integer, so the sum reassociates exactly and the
# group runs as a loop on every engine. A hundred thousand frames is ten times
# what the guard allows and a depth no engine's stack would hold.
fn weigh 0
  0

fn weigh n
  n + weigh (n - 1)

pub play = print "sum {weigh 100000}"
